#Title far ridge
#Composer SuperForge (procedural)
#Tempo 44

; heathaze's own song, and the FOURTH in the tree. The other three are spoken
; for and none of them is this: `slice_b_song` is the room rail's ambient
; piece, whose composed rest half-bar is the window its reverb A/B is measured
; in; `drive_song` is the action rails' D minor; `circuit_song` is a racing
; piece with a kit under it. This rail is a picture of HEAT AND DISTANCE, and
; what a picture of distance wants is a tune with very little in it.
;
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE HARMONY, AND WHY THIS ONE
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; D MIXOLYDIAN WITH A FLATTENED SIXTH: D E F+ G A B- C. The brief offered two
; colours and this takes both, which is the whole reason for choosing it over
; a plain minor.
;
;   * The MAJOR THIRD (f+) sitting over a bare D-A fifth is the "wide open"
;     sound — no minor third to close the interval down, and the drone under
;     it is a fifth rather than a triad so nothing fills the middle in.
;   * The FLAT SIXTH (b-) is the ache. It is where the tune goes at bar 7 and
;     it is where the DRONE ITSELF goes with it — the pedal drops from D-A to
;     B--F for two bars, which is the cheapest way to make a scale degree
;     audible as a colour instead of as a passing note.
;   * The FLAT SEVENTH (c) is what stops it resolving. There is no leading
;     tone anywhere in the piece, so the melody never arrives; it just stops.
;     That is what a horizon sounds like.
;
; Written as `#KeySignature +f, -b` so the mode is stated ONCE rather than
; spelled out with an accidental on every bar — circuit_song's argument for
; its own signature, applied to a scale that needs both a sharp and a flat.
;
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE ECHO HEADER IS NOT ENTIRELY THIS SONG'S TO CHOOSE
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; EVOL 12 / EFB 24 is a TREE-WIDE CONSTANT. `room_a_ambience` and `beam_end`
; write those two values back as LITERALS as the dry rest state, so a song
; whose header disagrees leaves any rail using those effects in a state it was
; never in, permanently, after the first use. drive_song was authored at 10/20
; and a red landing gate is how that was found. The BUFFER and the FIR are
; this song's own, because nothing writes them back: 128 ms is the longest
; delay the driver has, and the FIR is heavily low-passed, so the tail is a
; dull far-off return rather than a bright room. Taps sum to 127 — the
; compiler refuses an absolute sum over 128.
#EchoLength 128
#EchoVolume 12
#EchoFeedback 24
#FirFilter 56 40 20 8 3 0 0 0

#KeySignature +f, -b

@1 square_lead
@2 tri_bass
@3 pluck
@4 kick

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; NOTHING IS SCORED ON G OR H. Those are the two channels the audio driver
; DUCKS for the duration of any sound effect, and this rail now makes two
; kinds of noise — a wind bed on a cadence and a toggle cue on B — so a part
; written there would be gone for most of the drive rather than occasionally.
; A-F only, and F is left silent on purpose: see the arrangement note below.
;
; THE PERCUSSION IS A SAMPLE, NOT `N<0-31>`. There is exactly one noise
; generator and the driver zeroes the volume of every music channel sharing it
; the moment an effect wants it (audio-driver.asm, the `SfxNoise` branch) —
; and the `wind` effect wants it roughly every eighty frames. A noise part
; here would drop out under its own rail's weather.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; The heartbeat: a lub-dub on the downbeat and then three beats of nothing.
; K0 disables key-off — `kick` is a one-shot that decays to zero on its own
; and a key-off would choke its tail — so the gap is a `w` wait and never an
; `r` rest, which still keys off under K0.
!hb v9 o3 c%8 v6 c%8 w%80

; The punctuation: an open fifth, plucked, at the end of a phrase. Two notes
; a fifth apart rather than a chord, for the reason the whole piece is built
; that way — a third in the middle would close the interval.
!pl o3 d4 a4

; ---- A: the tune. One line, long notes, wide leaps, and more rest than note.
; WHISTLED, WHICH IS AN ENVELOPE AND A VIBRATO RATHER THAN A SAMPLE. MP's
; DELAY is the whole trick: 12 ticks pass before the vibrato starts, so a
; short note is a plain tone and a held one blooms into it — which is what a
; whistle does and what a plucked or bowed instrument does not.
;
; THE SLIDES ARE `{ }` PORTAMENTO with a delay, so the note SITS before it
; bends: `{d c}2,4` is d for a quarter and then a slide to c across the rest
; of the half. Three of them, all downward, all at the end of a phrase.
;
; EVERY BAR CARRIES ITS OWN OCTAVE, so a `>` inside one cannot leak into the
; next — the class of bug that is silent in MML and obvious on the chip.
A  L @1 v10 E1 Q8 MP18,l16,l8
A  r1                              | r2 o4 d4 e4
A  o4 f+2. a4                      | o5 d2 ^4 r4
A  r1                              | o5 {d c}2,4 o4 a4 r4
A  o4 b-1                          | o4 a2 r2
A  r1                              | r2 o4 a4 >c4
A  o5 d2. o4 b-4                   | o5 {c a}1,4
A  o4 g2 f+2                       | o4 e1
A  o4 {e d}2,4 r2                  | r1

; ---- B and C: the drone, as a BARE FIFTH held on two channels.
; One channel cannot hold two notes, and a broken chord flutters — so the
; open fifth this piece sits on costs two voices and that is what they are
; for. B is the root, C the fifth above it. Both dry: an echoed pedal smears
; into the melody's own tail and the two stop being separable.
;
; The pedal MOVES three times in sixteen bars and that is the entire form:
; D-A for six, B--F for the two the tune spends on the flat sixth, D-A for
; four, G-D for two, D-A to close. A drone that never moves is a texture; one
; that moves four times is a piece.
B  L @2 v9  E0 A15,0,7,0 p52
B  o2 d1 | o2 d1 | o2 d1 | o2 d1 | o2 d1 | o2 d1
B  o2 b-1 | o2 b-1
B  o2 d1 | o2 d1 | o2 d1 | o2 d1
B  o2 g1 | o2 g1
B  o2 d1 | o2 d1

C  L @2 v7  E0 A15,0,7,0 p76
C  o2 a1 | o2 a1 | o2 a1 | o2 a1 | o2 a1 | o2 a1
C  o3 f1 | o3 f1
C  o2 a1 | o2 a1 | o2 a1 | o2 a1
C  o3 d1 | o3 d1
C  o2 a1 | o2 a1

; ---- D: the plucked fifth, four times in the loop and nowhere else.
; It lands in the second half of every fourth bar, which is where the tune is
; already resting — so it fills a silence rather than competing with a line.
D  L @3 v6 E1 p96
D  r1 | r1 | r1 | r2 !pl
D  r1 | r1 | r1 | r2 !pl
D  r1 | r1 | r1 | r2 !pl
D  r1 | r1 | r1 | r2 !pl

; ---- E: the heartbeat.   ---- F: deliberately silent.
; F is empty because the arrangement is finished at five voices and a sixth
; would be filling. The two SFX channels are G and H, so an idle F costs
; nothing and buys the option of a part later without re-scoring anything.
E  L @4 K0 E0
E  [!hb ]16
